1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:02,785
This is DAO Treasury Analyst.

2
00:00:02,785 --> 00:00:07,230
You ask a question about a DAO's treasury in plain English.

3
00:00:07,230 --> 00:00:17,327
You get an answer where every number was computed by tested code, checked before you see it, and linked to the transactions behind it.

4
00:00:17,327 --> 00:00:24,560
In the next few minutes I'll show how it works, and how I measured whether it is actually right.

5
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:27,333
Here is the problem.

6
00:00:27,333 --> 00:00:37,086
If you paste a treasury's raw transactions into a language model and ask for total outflows in 2024, it answers confidently.

7
00:00:37,086 --> 00:00:42,198
In my evaluation it said nine hundred twenty-five sextillion UNI.

8
00:00:42,198 --> 00:00:46,288
It forgot that token amounts have eighteen decimals.

9
00:00:46,288 --> 00:00:50,142
The real answer is about two point three million.

10
00:00:50,142 --> 00:00:56,120
For treasury reporting, a fluent answer is worthless if the number is wrong.

11
00:00:57,320 --> 00:00:59,775
It starts with data I can trust.

12
00:00:59,775 --> 00:01:13,970
The pipeline pulls the full history of the Uniswap governance timelock from Etherscan, an archive node and DefiLlama prices, and pins it at one block, the end of June twenty twenty-six.

13
00:01:13,970 --> 00:01:18,803
Every response is recorded, so the whole thing replays offline.

14
00:01:18,803 --> 00:01:25,478
As a check, I rebuilt the balances from the transfers and compared them with the chain.

15
00:01:25,478 --> 00:01:26,936
They match exactly.

16
00:01:26,936 --> 00:01:33,842
Tokens are verified by contract address, so a spam token that calls itself UNI is ignored.

17
00:01:33,842 --> 00:01:40,440
And every counterparty label has a source; anything I couldn't verify stays unlabeled.

18
00:01:41,640 --> 00:01:43,657
Here is the architecture.

19
00:01:43,657 --> 00:01:49,545
The language model never sees raw transactions and never does arithmetic.

20
00:01:49,545 --> 00:01:57,691
It plans calls to typed tools: balances, flows, rankings, period comparisons and transaction details.

21
00:01:57,691 --> 00:02:00,757
The tools compute with exact integers.

22
00:02:00,757 --> 00:02:09,145
The model then submits its answer as structured claims, and each claim names the tool call it came from.

23
00:02:09,145 --> 00:02:15,840
A verifier checks every one of those numbers in code before the user sees anything.

24
00:02:17,040 --> 00:02:18,813
Here is a real answer.

25
00:02:18,813 --> 00:02:24,214
I ask how much UNI the treasury has sent to the Uniswap Foundation.

26
00:02:24,214 --> 00:02:32,195
The agent calls three tools, and answers: thirteen point one four million UNI, in two transactions.

27
00:02:32,195 --> 00:02:37,596
Both transactions are linked, so anyone can open them on Etherscan.

28
00:02:37,596 --> 00:02:44,609
The dollar value comes with a caveat: it uses a pinned price snapshot, not live prices.

29
00:02:44,609 --> 00:02:48,640
Both claims were verified against the tool output.

30
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:51,474
This is the verifier.

31
00:02:51,474 --> 00:02:57,076
A claim is a value, a unit, the call it came from, and its transactions.

32
00:02:57,076 --> 00:03:10,693
The verifier finds that value in the stored tool result, checks the unit and the transaction hashes, and rejects any number written into the prose that isn't a verified claim.

33
00:03:10,693 --> 00:03:16,062
If something fails, the model gets one retry with the exact problems.

34
00:03:16,062 --> 00:03:20,497
If it fails again, the user only sees the verified parts.

35
00:03:20,497 --> 00:03:26,800
A number the model computed itself is rejected, even when it happens to be right.

36
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:31,189
Just as important is knowing when to say no.

37
00:03:31,189 --> 00:03:34,884
It refuses investment advice and price predictions.

38
00:03:34,884 --> 00:03:44,378
It refuses to look up wallets that aren't the treasury or a verified institution, because that could mean profiling private people.

39
00:03:44,378 --> 00:03:52,712
And when the data simply isn't there, like a date after the snapshot, it abstains and says exactly what is missing.

40
00:03:52,712 --> 00:03:56,480
These are real recorded answers from the evaluation.

41
00:03:57,680 --> 00:03:59,004
Then I measured it.

42
00:03:59,004 --> 00:04:04,440
I wrote eighty-five questions, including twenty-three that should be declined.

43
00:04:04,440 --> 00:04:14,546
The expected answers come from a separate SQL script that shares no code with the tools, and the two agree on every figure they can both compute.

44
00:04:14,546 --> 00:04:24,791
I compared three setups with the same model: a naive baseline that reads the raw transactions, the tools without the verifier, and the full system.

45
00:04:24,791 --> 00:04:31,760
Every model call is recorded, so anyone can replay the evaluation offline and get identical numbers.

46
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:34,458
Here are the results.

47
00:04:34,458 --> 00:04:43,800
The naive baseline answered six and a half percent of the answerable questions correctly, and wrongly gave up on a quarter of them.

48
00:04:43,800 --> 00:04:50,361
With typed tools, accuracy jumps to almost eighty-nine percent, using a tenth of the tokens.

49
00:04:50,361 --> 00:04:54,640
Advice requests and unknown wallets were refused every time.

50
00:04:55,840 --> 00:05:00,677
There's also a finding I didn't expect, and I report it as it is.

51
00:05:00,677 --> 00:05:03,355
The verifier did not raise accuracy.

52
00:05:03,355 --> 00:05:07,299
Once the model had tools, it never invented a number.

53
00:05:07,299 --> 00:05:19,428
The verifier rejected two correct sums the model had computed itself, and its prose check flagged harmless digits, like the four hundred four in a delegate's name.

54
00:05:19,428 --> 00:05:26,720
So its value is a guarantee, not a score: every figure a user sees can be traced to a tool result.

55
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:31,218
The error analysis pointed to four fixes.

56
00:05:31,218 --> 00:05:41,436
To avoid tuning on the test, I evaluated them on thirty-two new held-out questions, and ran the old code on the same questions.

57
00:05:41,436 --> 00:05:50,124
Accuracy went from seventy-six to eighty-eight percent, and correct refusals from eighty-three to a hundred.

58
00:05:50,124 --> 00:05:56,560
It's a small sample, so I traced every changed answer to the fix that caused it.

59
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:05,054
You can use it from the command line, or as a Telegram bot, and it ships with Docker for a small server.

60
00:06:05,054 --> 00:06:12,839
The model provider is swappable, and pointing it at another DAO or another EVM chain is a configuration change.

61
00:06:12,839 --> 00:06:18,240
Every question, tool call and verification result is written to an audit log.

62
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:22,343
And you don't have to trust me either.

63
00:06:22,343 --> 00:06:32,960
Clone the repository, and three commands rebuild the data, replay the full evaluation and run all the tests, offline, without any API keys.

64
00:06:34,160 --> 00:06:41,660
That's DAO Treasury Analyst: typed tools, verified claims, and an evaluation you can rerun yourself.

65
00:06:41,660 --> 00:06:47,360
If you run a DAO and want this for your treasury, it's a config change away.

66
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:50,360
The code and every result are on GitHub.
